    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm putting together a brief for our stakeholders about our infrastructure plan. Can you summarize the main differences between vertical scaling and horizontal scaling for someone who isn't a dev/engineer? Keep it professional, thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling means upgrading the specifications of existing servers, such as adding more memory or thicker storage discs, to handle increased demand. In contrast, horizontal scaling involves adding more identical servers to the network to distribute the workload across a broader pool of resources. While vertical scaling improves power for a single machine, horizontal scaling creates a more flexible and fault-tolerant system that can grow by simply adding more components. Both strategies are vital for ensuring your infrastructure remains stable as user numbers increase.",
