    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you dive a bit deeper into the downsides of each? Specifically, I want to explain to them why we can't just keep vertically scaling a single server forever and what the trade-offs are when we eventually move to horizontal scaling. Maybe mention the complexity side of it too.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling hits a physical limit once hardware manufacturing reaches its maximum capacity, making it costly and risky to upgrade individual servers indefinitely without system downtime. Once you exceed those physical limits, your infrastructure becomes a single point of failure, which negates the fault tolerance benefits you gain from moving to horizontal scaling. Shifting to a horizontally scaled architecture also introduces significant operational complexity, requiring management teams to master distributed systems, data replication, and consistent networking across many servers rather than just a single physical machine.",
      "source": "target",
