    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, the complexity part is exactly what they'll be worried about. Is there any way to talk about those \"hidden\" or unexpected issues that pop up in a distributed system—like things breaking in ways that are hard to track down or unexpected load spikes causing weird errors? I want to make sure they understand that it's not just \"more servers = better,\" but that there's a lot of unpredictability involved.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Distributed systems often introduce \"hidden\" failures where data inconsistency or network latency causes errors that are difficult to diagnose because they occur across multiple non-centralized components. Additionally, unexpected load spikes can expose architectural weaknesses, leading to cascading failures that are far harder to manage than a simple server crash. This unpredictability means that while horizontal scaling offers flexibility, it requires rigorous monitoring and robust testing to handle these complex, non-linear issues effectively.",
      "source": "target",
